{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779457020,"nonce":"O4EYhIZAH9bcgJD6tizUTia3eqI=","message":"## Updated framing: ValueSpec scope eligibility and backend lowering\n\nFollow-up design note from the current Studio planning thread: the generalized ValueSpec editor needs an explicit eligibility layer over both field kind and variation scope. It is not enough to say that a field can hold a constant/function/distribution/schedule; the valid modes depend on whether the variation happens across runs, replicates, trials, phases, or rollout timesteps.\n\nProposed scope/field rule:\n\n- Sweep across training runs: may vary almost anything, including topology, component type, hidden size, task type, objective set, optimizer family, and other shape/static choices. The sweep expands into distinct run specs/manifests and may require separate compilation.\n- Single run config: may choose one concrete graph, model topology, dimensions, task, objective, and static parameter set before compile/execution.\n- Replicate/ensemble scope: must preserve topology and PyTree shapes because this is vmap-like. It can vary same-shaped numeric leaves, seeds, initial states, task samples, and runtime controls, but not hidden size, component class, or graph structure.\n- Trial/episode scope: also shape-preserving and batch/vmap-like. It can vary targets, cues, perturbation amplitudes, sampled task params, masks, and other same-shaped trial data.\n- Rollout timestep scope: also shape/topology-preserving. It should lower to per-step signals, masks, task data, explicit control ports, intervention params_override inputs, or scan-compatible arrays/functions, not mutation of Equinox module fields.\n\nBackend implication:\n\n- Equinox modules remain immutable; structural/static changes create new module trees out of place, e.g. via `eqx.tree_at`, and belong at run/sweep materialization time.\n- Trainable numeric module leaves are updated by producing new module trees during optimization, not in-place mutation.\n- Runtime-varying controls should be data flowing through `lax.scan`: task inputs, explicit graph ports, intervention/control ports, or precomputed `(T, ...)` arrays/functions.\n- Persistent evolving values belong in state when they are genuinely stateful, such as delay buffers, recurrent state, running normalization stats, or component-owned runtime state. A schedule indexed by `t` is usually input/control data, not state.\n\nUX implication:\n\nEvery editable value chip should expose allowed modes according to this eligibility layer. If a user asks for an illegal scope, Studio should explain why and offer a promotion path where possible: for example, `Promote to task signal`, `Promote to control/intervention input`, or `Use as run sweep`. A field like `hidden_size` can be swept across runs, but cannot vary by replicate/trial/timestep inside one compiled vmapped/scan execution.\n\nThis does not change the umbrella intent here; it sharpens the acceptance criteria for the core ValueSpec schema/lowering slice and should be split into child issues before implementation.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}